      "availability": "Auditing policy dependent",
      "availability_flags": ["CONFIG", "POLICY", "EXPORT"],
      "configuration_required": true,
      "configuration_notes": "Enable 'Turn on PowerShell Script Block Logging' through Group Policy (Windows Components > Windows PowerShell for 5.1; Administrative Templates > PowerShell Core for 7), the policy registry key, or the MDM Policy CSP (WindowsPowerShell/TurnOnPowerShellScriptBlockLogging). On Windows, PowerShell 7 also needs its event provider registered ($PSHOME\\RegisterManifest.ps1). Microsoft recommends Protected Event Logging when script block logging is used for more than diagnostics. Central collection: an Azure Monitor Agent data collection rule with a Windows events data source sends the selected events to the Event table. Custom filters use the form LogName!XPath (XPath 1.0 only); test a filter locally with Get-WinEvent -FilterXPath first. Analytic and Debug channels can't be collected, and event logs must be on a local disk. Example filter, built from the documented format: Microsoft-Windows-PowerShell/Operational!*[System[(EventID=4104)]].",
